<p>Like many others I was saddened to hear that Patrick Stewart had died. Star Trek: TNG was how I first came to know him in my teens, and I believe it&rsquo;s not an exaggeration to say that I loved whatever characters he played or voiced.</p>
<p>As an adult, I grew to admire him further, especially after I learned he&rsquo;d moved relatively close to me — to <a href="https://web.archive.org/web/20121004232309/http://www.businessinsider.com/patrick-stewart-buys-in-park-slope-2012-10">Park Slope in Brooklyn</a> (at least when he was in New York). I used to work in that neighborhood, and I remember thinking that maybe I&rsquo;d run into him one day. The <a href="https://web.archive.org/web/20261030195111/https://nypost.com/2026/10/29/real-estate/patrick-stewart-quietly-sold-nyc-home-a-year-before-his-death/">house has since sold</a> to an unknown buyer — probably a friend or a relative.</p>
<p>I didn&rsquo;t know about his deep friendship with Ian McKellen until X-Men, when I suspected the two had a certain chemistry on the screen. I was excited and felt dumb at the same time when I learned that my hunch was a well-known fact to everyone other than me. I looked into it more and learned that the two <a href="https://mckellen.com/stage/godot/index.htm">played together in Waiting for Godot on Broadway</a>, and I&rsquo;ve been trying to get a copy of that production for a long time. A recording of it is <a href="https://www.nypl.org/locations/lpa/theatre-film-and-tape-archive">available for viewing only at the New York Public Library</a>, by appointment.</p>
<p>As a fan of the old TNG show (I almost finished a rewatch a couple of years ago), I am thinking of giving <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Star_Trek:_Deep_Space_Nine">DS9</a> another try. It was a show I started watching but never got into. Maybe I&rsquo;ll watch First Contact. And of course, there&rsquo;s <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Star_Trek:_Picard">Picard</a>, the show about the captain himself, and from what I gather it &ldquo;requires&rdquo; that I watch some of the Star Trek films again. I might end up going that route.</p>
<p>Come to think of it, it was also him who got me to &ldquo;get serious&rdquo; about my Earl Grey. I always liked the tea, but after I started re-watching TNG it became a permanent staple on my coffee cart at home, along with Dragon Well, my favorite green tea. By now I know it was never really Earl Grey anyway (he liked Yorkshire Gold), but the habit stuck. Maybe I&rsquo;ll try some.</p>
<p>I miss him. I&rsquo;m grateful for his influence.</p>
